export const LESSONS={"start": "<p class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 10 \u00b7 LEARNING PROTOTYPE</p><h1>Make the case.</h1><p>Produce a 600\u2013800-word research memo and an exportable evidence record. Answer: does the predefined rule improve the risk/return trade-off against its same-period benchmark after costs, and what remains uncertain?</p><div class=\"panel\"><h2>Your 180-minute route</h2><p>Orientation 20 \u00b7 plan 20 \u00b7 calculations 35 \u00b7 memo 50 \u00b7 self-review 25 \u00b7 revision/export 30 minutes. Optional Python adds 35 minutes. Pacing and assessment effectiveness await human pilot validation.</p><p>OBJ-M10 / CLO-06: assemble reproducible evidence; distinguish observation from a forecast; meet every critical integrity item; self-assess against the analytic rubric and revise the memo. Profit is not a marking criterion.</p></div><p>Standalone path: the prepared plan below supplies the rule, costs, benchmark and evaluation block. If you completed Chapter 9, open its exported record beside this page and copy your question/prediction, including prior-inspection disclosure. There is no silent cross-chapter import. If you already saw evaluation results, disclose that the exercise is retrospective/exploratory. A fresh local plan is not proof of statistical preregistration.</p>", "plan": "<h1>A bounded plan you can reproduce.</h1><p>Use the prepared plan or copy your Chapter 9 question and prediction. You must freeze it here before ordinary-UI reveal, even on the standalone path. Public source files can expose the data: this is a learning gate, not secure blindness.</p><p>Rule: compare current close with the mean of the last three closes; position is long when strictly above, otherwise cash. Execute next open, earn the following open-to-open return. Start \u00a31,000; cash zero; no shorting/leverage. Base 10bps and predefined 30bps cost sensitivity per traded notional. Benchmark same eligible dates and capital, entry and final exit costs. No dividends, taxes, extra slippage or liquidity model.</p><p>Dataset qf-synthetic-evaluation-2026-v1 is an original deterministic synthetic sequence, Feb 1\u201316 2026; it is not historical market evidence. First three closes are warmup. Edits invalidate current evidence and require a new frozen version; inspected revised plans are exploratory and old attempts remain.</p>", "evidence": "<h1>Keep the result traceable.</h1><p>Reveal both cost cases. Hand-check the first eligible row before writing the memo: start \u00d7 (1 \u2212 turnover \u00d7 bps/10,000) \u00d7 market factor. For a long position, market factor = next exit open / entry open; for cash it is 1. Terminal liquidation follows the last row. Export contains the full input data, plan versions, every trial and row-level results. Report total simple return = final wealth / initial \u2212 1, with no annualisation.</p><p>For a discussion of risk, compare the displayed positive maximum-drawdown magnitudes for strategy and benchmark at both cost levels. Drawdown=100×(1−wealth/runningpeak); include initial£1,000, period-end wealth and final liquidation. A simple100→80→100path has20%maximum drawdown even though it ends unchanged. These are observed boundary losses, not intraperiod or future bounds. Do not imply that a higher final value proves a better risk-adjusted investment. This compact capstone assesses traceability, timing and judgment, not estimation of a stable Sharpe ratio from a tiny synthetic sample.</p>", "memo": "<h1>Draft 600\u2013800 words.</h1><p>Suggested scaffold: question and decision (80\u2013100 words); source and chronology (100\u2013130); method and matching benchmark/costs (130\u2013160); calculation evidence and both cost cases (130\u2013160); limitations and what would change the decision (100\u2013150); conclusion and revision note (60\u2013100). These are guides, not separate hard word quotas.</p><p>Include dataset/version, rule and timing, frozen-plan history and prior exposure; first-row hand calculation; base and sensitivity net wealth/returns with same-period benchmark; every trial, including negative results; at least three consequential limits; a conditional decision. Place full rows and trial log in the exported calculation attachment rather than pasting every row into prose.</p><p>Word count checks assignment length only. It cannot judge truthful sourcing, reasoning or writing quality. The criterion checklist and rubric below are learner self-assessment, not external marking.</p>", "review": "<h1>Calibrate the judgment.</h1><p><strong>Calibration A \u2014 rejection can earn full marks:</strong> a memo reports the versioned synthetic data and all trials, correctly hand-checks costs, uses next-open/matched dates, shows both net cases, and rejects deployment because one tiny synthetic period and simplified execution provide insufficient evidence. It explains three specific limits and revises a claim in response. If all details are traceable, the rubric permits 20+25+25+20+10=100; rejection earns no penalty.</p><p><strong>Calibration B \u2014 profitable but unacceptable:</strong> \u201cOur 18% proves it always wins\u201d appears without a traceable calculation; the signal earns an earlier return, losers are omitted and the benchmark uses different dates. Illustrative scores: source 5/20, calculation 0/25, design 0/25, limits 0/20, clarity 2/10 = 7/100. Critical timing, traceability and selection defects block readiness regardless of prose polish.</p><p>These are illustrative anchored examples, not independent grader calibration. Compare your actual memo with each criterion. Self-score honestly; cite a passage or row supporting your judgment and identify a concrete revision. A 70-point self-score plus every critical item is a prototype readiness check, not a validated course grade or credential.</p>", "finish": "<h1>Revise the memo, then export.</h1><p>After recording a self-review, return to the memo and change at least one claim or explanation. Here explain the change, its rubric criterion and supporting evidence. A revision note without a changed memo is insufficient.</p><p>The exported record contains all frozen plans, runs, self-reviews and revisions plus source data. Save a separate plain-text memo and the evidence JSON. Anyone reproducing it should use the named dataset, fixed rule and both costs; compare first-row arithmetic and final wealth. Exports do not import or synchronise progress. No coursewide qualification is automatically awarded.</p>"};
export const SOURCES=[["Bailey et al., Probability of Backtest Overfitting","https://www.davidhbailey.com/dhbpapers/backtest-prob.pdf"],["Investor.gov, investment fees","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/understanding-fees"]];
